    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
    <div class="takeaways">
      <div class="takeaway">
        <h4>Feature Engineering Matters Most</h4>
        <p>The 10+ percentage point improvement from state-based time-series features over outcome-based features demonstrates that <strong>what is measured matters more than which algorithm is chosen</strong>. Proper feature engineering produced larger gains than algorithm selection.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway">
        <h4>Economy Dominates Round Outcomes</h4>
        <p>Equipment advantage emerged as the single strongest predictor across all methods. This quantitatively confirms that <strong>winning the economy war is the most controllable factor</strong> in competitive CS2.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway">
        <h4>Ensemble Methods Outperform Simple Models</h4>
        <p>Boosting methods (AdaBoost, XGBoost, Gradient Boosting) significantly outperformed individual decision trees and Naive Bayes classifiers, demonstrating the power of combining multiple weak learners.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway">
        <h4>More Data Enables Better Learning</h4>
        <p>The increase in dataset size to 2,507 samples through time-series sampling enabled Gradient Boosting to achieve 86.65% accuracy with 0.942 ROC AUC, demonstrating the importance of sufficient training data.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

